{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Archaeology: the gate EXISTS — the gap is sanction granularity\n`scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py` (policy d37e-4f64-3265-4f30 / empirical-inhuman-urus, landed 6739ea54fa, wired in `_build-and-test.yml:172`) already gates raw git writes: AST layer P over src/+scripts, workflow layer W, inline `# raw-git-ok: <reason>` sanction, fail-closed opaque-argv. It passes CLEAN on the current tree — so the hand-rolled reconciler operations a parallel session found are NOT violations under its policy.\n\nWhy it missed them: the policy sanctions the reconciler seam at MODULE granularity — all of `git_adapter.py` and `_ref_lock.py` are \"write seam internal\". `git_adapter` exports low-level PRIMITIVES (`add`, `commit`, `commit_email`, `commit_tree`) as sanctioned wrappers, so any caller may COMPOSE them into a hand-rolled non-atomic sequence and the gate approves every call. Proven instance: `reconcile_helpers.py:226` (`git_adapter.add`) + `:246` (`git_adapter.commit`) — inline stage+commit against the tickets tracker with no lock and no atomic commit+push transaction. The gate enforces the MECHANISM rule (\"no raw subprocess git\") but not the PROTOCOL rule (\"tracker mutations go through the atomic locked commit+push transaction\"), so bypasses of the atomic seam are invisible to it by design.\n\n## Fix: add a composition rule (R3) to the existing checker — not a new checker\n1. In `check_raw_git_writes.py`, add rule R3: a call to a sanctioned git-wrapper module's MUTATION PRIMITIVES (`add`, `commit`, `commit_email`, `commit_tree`, ref-planting writes) from OUTSIDE the atomic-transaction functions fires, unless the call site carries `# raw-git-ok:` with a reason. The sanctioned entry points become the atomic transaction seams themselves (the locked write-transaction functions in `_store` and the reconciler's `_ref_lock` transaction), not every export of the wrapper modules.\n2. Enumerate today's out-of-transaction composition sites from the new rule's first run; sanction each with an inline reasoned marker referencing the parallel session's remediation ticket, so the debt is visible in-tree and drains as that session lands (marker removed with each fix).\n3. Keep the existing marker/fail-closed semantics — no allowlist file, reason mandatory.\n4. Tests in `tests/unit/test_raw_git_write_lint.py`: composed add+commit outside a transaction fires R3; the same calls inside the sanctioned transaction functions do not; marker-with-reason sanctions; marker-without-reason fails.\n\n## Non-goals\nRemediating the existing composition instances (the parallel session owns those; our markers reference their ticket); changing git_adapter's API; runtime interception.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] R3 exists in `scripts/check_raw_git_writes.py`: out-of-transaction calls to sanctioned wrapper mutation primitives are flagged; sanctioned-transaction internals are not.\n- [ ] Today's composition sites are enumerated by the rule and carry reasoned inline markers referencing the remediation ticket (no silent allowlist).\n- [ ] Unit tests cover: R3 fires on composed add/commit outside a transaction; does not fire inside sanctioned transaction functions; marker semantics (reasoned passes, reasonless fails).\n- [ ] Checker passes on the current tree with the markers in place; CI wiring unchanged (`_build-and-test.yml` already runs it).\n- [ ] `make lint` / `make typecheck` clean; lands via Gerrit (LLM-Review +1, Verified +1)."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786808292136242001,"uuid":"b85241b9-191f-4712-960a-31b1eeb8f0cd"}